INT. STRANDED BUS ON FLOODED CAUSEWAY - NIGHT

A mid-size passenger bus, headlights still burning, surrounded by black water that laps at the doors. The aisle is wet to the ankle. Rain drums on the roof.

GWEN MARSDEN, fifties, the driver, grips the wheel though the engine is dead. HIRO YAMAMOTO, forties, a salesman with a leather briefcase, stands in the aisle. OLUWASEUN, twenties, sits on the back bench with her knees drawn up. AUNTIE LUPE, seventies, wraps a shawl around a covered basket. BARNABY, ten, sits very still beside her.

GWEN
Everybody stay on the seats. Above the water line.

HIRO
(to Gwen)
How far is the next village?

GWEN
Two miles.

HIRO
In which direction?

GWEN
Away from the water.

Hiro lifts his briefcase to his chest. He sets it down. He lifts it again.

AUNTIE LUPE
(to Barnaby)
Eat something, my darling.

BARNABY
I'm not hungry.

AUNTIE LUPE
Eat.

Barnaby takes a roll from the basket and holds it in both hands without biting it. Oluwaseun shifts her feet up onto the seat.

OLUWASEUN
(to herself)
Okay. Okay. Okay.

HIRO
Did somebody say something?

OLUWASEUN
No.

The bus tilts a few degrees. Everyone grabs a seatback. A cold line of water slides along the floor.

GWEN
(to the bus)
Nobody panic. Everybody move to the front.

They move. Hiro hesitates, then offers Auntie Lupe his arm. She takes it without a word. Barnaby sets down the roll and picks up the basket.

AUNTIE LUPE
Leave it, darling.

BARNABY
It's heavy.

AUNTIE LUPE
Then we carry it together.
